<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</h1><p class="st-meta">1621 CE – 1695 CE · capital Güstrow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North German duchy that briefly passed to Wallenstein during the Thirty Years' War, ruled from a fine Renaissance palace at Güstrow.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> had already been divided before, but the partition of 1621 between the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-frederick-i-duke-of-mecklenburg">Adolf Frederick I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg">John Albert II</a> created a lasting Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> in the east of the country, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> in the west. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rostock">Rostock</a> and the estates remained shared between the two duchies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Both dukes were deposed in 1628 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Ferdinand II</a>, who gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> to his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>; Wallenstein governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> until Swedish troops restored the dukes in 1631. Government was weakened by powerful noble estates, and the countryside suffered heavily from war and depopulation. The duchy was Lutheran.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolph-duke-of-mecklenburg-gustrow">Gustav Adolph</a>, the last duke, died without male heirs in 1695. A long succession dispute followed until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hamburg-1701">Treaty of Hamburg</a> in 1701 divided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-mecklenburg-gustrow.jpg" alt="Duchy of Mecklenburg-Güstrow" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Wilhelm Putzger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mecklenburg_1648_(135770347).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1621 CE – 1695 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Güstrow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Albert II, Albrecht von Wallenstein, Gustav Adolph</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mecklenburg-Güstrow</dd></dl></aside></div>
